          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A question that is traditionally of some debate in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            is,
          %blockquote
            %p
              What does it mean for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
              to have more than one
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
          %p This question is relevant in a number of situations:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the interpretation of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polymorphism"} polymorphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtyping"} subtyping
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
          %h2#subtyping Subtyping
          %p
            One place where the above question is relevant is for type systems with a non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtyping"} subtyping
            relation, in which case it becomes pertinent to consider the meaning of the
            %em subsumption
            rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{e:A \qquad A \le B}{e:B}
          %p
            One possibility (sometimes called the
            %strong inclusion interpretation
            of subtyping) is based on the idea of interpreting types as
            %em properties
            of terms (what is also sometimes called “types à la Curry”, or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intrinsic+and+extrinsic+views+of+typing"} extrinsic
            view of typing). Under this interpretation, the subsumption rule can be read more or less literally, as asserting that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            satisfies the property
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            entails
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            also satisfies the property
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
            by
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+object"} reflexive object
          %p
            A different possibility (sometimes called the
            %strong coercion interpretation
            of subtyping) is to read the subsumption rule as introducing an implicit
            %em coercion
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intrinsic+and+extrinsic+views+of+typing"} intrinsic
            (“à la Church”) view of typing, but on the other hand it introduces a subtle issue of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em coherence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \le B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %h3#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p
            Consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A: Type, x:A \vdash P(x):Prop
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{x:A} P(x)
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong coerce
            the identification.
          %p
            For example, we might have introduced ‘Eve’ as a term of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Woman
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Woman
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relatives
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Eve
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relatives(Eve)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relatives
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relatives(x)
            had been introduced as a type depending on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi Human
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: Human
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Woman
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Woman
            had been defined as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi Human
                    %mi Female
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{x:Human} Female(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eve
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eve
            has projections to the underlying human and to a warrant for Eve’s being female. It would be clumsy to insist on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relatives
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Eve
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relatives(\pi_1(Eve))
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relatives
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Eve
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relatives(Eve)
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Luo99"} Luo (1999)
            proposed a way of formalizing coercions in dependent type theory, through what he calls the
            = succeed "," do
              %em coercive definition rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi c
                      %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma, x: B \vdash f(x) : C(x) \; \; \; \Gamma \vdash a : A \;\;\;\Gamma \vdash A \lt_{c} B : Type}{\Gamma \vdash f(a) = f(c(a)) : C(c(a))}
          %p
            In this notation, a coercion between two types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi c
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \lt_{c} B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c: (A \to B)
          %h3#in_hott In HoTT
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe in type theory
            in a larger one is often considered as a coercion.
          %h3#coherence Coherence
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Reynolds00"} Reynolds (2000)
            gave an interesting proof of coherence for a coercion interpretation of a language with subtyping. The proof works by first building a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+relation"} logical relation
            between this “intrinsic semantics” and a separate untyped semantics of the language (the latter based on interpreting programs as elements of a universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+theory"} domain
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [[A]]
            in the image of the intrinsic semantics, there are a pair of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_A : [[A]] \to U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_A : U \to [[A]]
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              for any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in [[A]]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is logically related to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_A(a)
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is logically related to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = \psi_A(x)
          %p
            Combined, the logical relations theorem and the bracketing theorem imply that the coercion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [[\alpha]] : [[A]] \to [[B]]
            associated to any derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            of a subtyping judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \le B
            can be factored as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ψ
                                %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{matrix}
                  [[A]] &amp;\overset{\phi_A}{\to} &amp; U &amp; \overset{\psi_B}{\to} &amp; [[B]]
                  \\end{matrix}
          %p
            and hence in particular that any two derivations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_1,\alpha_2
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \le B
            result in the same coercion
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ;
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [[\alpha_1]] = [[\alpha_2]] = (\phi_A;\psi_B) : [[A]] \to [[B]]
          %h2#judgmental_equality_of_types Judgmental equality of types
          %p
            Another place where the above question is relevant in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            which uses a separate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            of types: if two types are judgmentally equal, then every term of the first type is also a term of the second type. This is given from the element conversion rule of judgmental equality,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{e:A \qquad A \equiv B}{e:B}
          %p
            which is provable from the structural rules of judgmental equality (cf. section 1.1 &amp; exercise 1.1 of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Rijke22"} Rijke22
          %p
            Judgmental equality of types is the symmetric version of subtyping, since subtyping forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            on types while judgmental equality forms an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            on types, which is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+preorder"} symmetric preorder
          %p
            One possibility (sometimes called the
            %strong inclusion interpretation
            of judgmental equality) is based on the idea of interpreting types as
            %em properties
            of terms (what is also sometimes called “types à la Curry”, or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intrinsic+and+extrinsic+views+of+typing"} extrinsic
            view of typing). Under this interpretation, the element conversion rule can be read more or less literally, as asserting that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            satisfies the property
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is logically equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            also satisfies the property
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
            by
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+object"} reflexive object
          %p
            A different possibility (sometimes called the
            %strong coercion interpretation
            of judgmental equality) is to read the subsumption rule as introducing an implicit
            %em coercion
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intrinsic+and+extrinsic+views+of+typing"} intrinsic
            (“à la Church”) view of typing, but on the other hand it introduces a subtle issue of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em coherence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \equiv B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
